{"id":"98aa377c-ddfa-4104-a837-c6ca51470236","object":"chat.completion","created":1784986013,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not required_symbols:\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case structure')\\n    node_map = {}\\n    child_map = {}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        removable = node['removable']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            child_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[symbol] = symbol_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\\n    # root is the node with parent None\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed_ids = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed_ids:\\n            continue\\n        # never remove root\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        # must be removable\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        # required symbol nodes cannot be removed\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        # check retained children\\n        children = child_map.get(nid, [])\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid not in removed_ids for cid in children)\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        # tentative removal: check required symbols still present\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        new_count = symbol_counts[sym] - 1\\n        if sym in required_symbols and new_count == 0:\\n            continue\\n        # perform removal\\n        removed_ids.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = new_count\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids])\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_ids}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` based on the description. The case has `nodes` list and `required_symbols`. The task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - we need to reduce a parse tree by removing removable nodes (with `removable: true`) while preserving required symbols. The root is always present and not removable. The algorithm: consider deeper nodes first, then shallower; for nodes at same depth, tie-break by node ID (stable). Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented in the tree after removal. Also, cannot remove a node that has retained children (if children are retained, node must remain? Actually: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained (not removed), then that node cannot be removed because it serves as parent. Also, required symbols: nodes with symbol in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also, removable flag must be true. So we need to traverse nodes in a specific order: deeper first, then by depth ascending? \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process nodes in order of decreasing depth (deepest first). For ties, use node ID as tie-break (maybe sorted ascending? Not specified but stable tie break using node ID; likely alphabetical order). Then for each node we consider removal: if it is removable, not root, not required symbol, and all children are either removed or will be removed? Actually \"a node with retained children\" cannot be removed. So if any child is currently retained (i.e., not removed yet), then node cannot be removed. So we need to process in post-order (children before parent). The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That matches post-order (children first). So we can sort nodes by depth descending, and if same depth, by ID ascending (or descending? stable tie break: use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Probably ascending order.)\n\nThen for each node in that order, we check:\n- node['removable'] is True\n- node['id'] is not 'root' (or root id)\n- node['symbol'] not in required_symbols (note: required_symbols list may contain symbols, not IDs)\n- All children of this node that are still in the tree (i.e., not removed) are themselves removed? Actually \"a node with retained children\" cannot be removed. So we need to check if any child (nodes whose parent is this node's id) has not been removed yet. If there are any such children, then skip removal.\n- Also, after tentative removal, all required symbols must still be present in the tree (i.e., among retained nodes). So we need to check if any required symbol is only represented by this node (i.e., no other node with that symbol remains). But careful: multiple nodes can have same symbol. So we need to ensure that after removal, there is at least one node with each required symbol that is not removed. So we need to track counts of symbols among retained nodes.\n\nSimplify: start with all nodes not removed. Then iterate over sorted nodes. For each node, test removal: if condition satisfied, mark removed. Ensure we don't remove root or required symbol nodes. Also need to respect \"node with retained children\": so before removal, check if any child is still retained. Since we process deeper first, children will be processed before parent, so if a child was removed, that's fine; if child was retained (maybe because it was not removable or required), then parent cannot be removed.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build mapping from node id to node dict. Also build parent-child relationships.\n- Create set of all node ids.\n- Determine required symbols set.\n- Determine initial symbol counts: map symbol->count of nodes having that symbol.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (or descending? Let's assume ascending for tie-break as stable sort).\n- For each node in that order:\n  - if node['id'] == 'root' or not node['removable']: skip.\n  - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: skip (since required symbols cannot be removed).\n  - Check if any child (nodes whose parent is this node's id) is still not removed. If yes, skip.\n  - Tentatively remove: check if after removal, for all required symbols, count > 0. For this, we can compute symbol count after removal: decrement symbol count for node's symbol. If any required symbol count becomes 0, then removal invalid. If valid, actually remove: mark node as removed, add to removed list, update symbol count.\n- After processing, collect retained node ids (those not removed). Return sorted retained_node_ids and removed_node_ids (order of removal? The output says removed_node_ids array, but not specified order. In description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to return removed_node_ids in the order they were actually removed (i.e., the order we processed and removed). Retained_node_ids sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID order? \"sorted retained IDs\" - so sort them. Probably ascending.\n\nReturn object with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids (arrays of strings).\n\nEdge cases: If the input is structurally invalid (missing fields, etc.), raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation details in Python. Use only standard library. No random, no files.\n\nNow produce code that defines `solve(case)`. The function receives case payload (dict with keys 'nodes' and 'required_symbols' and possibly '_case_nonce' but ignore that). Must return dict with the exact keys.\n\nLet's write the code accordingly.\n\nImportant: Use node ID as tie breaker; for children, we need to know which nodes are children. We'll precompute children list for each node.\n\nAlso need to handle case where there is no root? Assume root exists.\n\nThe \"required symbols\" are the symbols that must be present at least once. The root's symbol is \"root\" but not necessarily in required_symbols? In examples, required_symbols include \"root\" and \"s1\". So root symbol is typically required. But the root node itself is not removable regardless.\n\nNow, note: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So even if a node's symbol is required, we skip.\n\nAlso, \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we check symbol counts after removal.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate payload: check keys 'nodes' and 'required_symbols', nodes is list, each node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (utility may be used? Not in algorithm, but we can ignore). If any missing, raise ValueError.\n2. Build node_dict by id.\n3. Build children mapping: dict id -> list of child ids.\n4. Build symbol_counts: defaultdict(int) from symbol to count.\n5. Create set of required_symbols.\n6. Create set removed_ids = set().\n7. Create list removal_order = [].\n8. Sort nodes list by (-depth, id) (descending depth, then ascending id).\n9. For each node in sorted list:\n   - if node['id'] in removed_ids: continue (already removed? shouldn't happen as we process each node once, but safe)\n   - if node['id'] == ??? We need to identify root. In examples, root id is \"root\". But could be other? The task says \"root\" but we can assume id is \"root\" from cases. But safer: check that parent is None for root. We'll treat root as the node with parent null. So skip if parent is None.\n   - if not node['removable']: continue.\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue.\n   - Check children: children = children_map.get(node['id'], []). For each child_id, if child_id not in removed_ids: then node has retained children -> skip.\n   - Tentative removal: symbol = node['symbol']; if symbol_counts[symbol] - 1 < 1 and symbol in required_symbols: not allowed? Actually we already checked required symbol nodes are skipped. But if a symbol appears in multiple nodes, removing one might still leave others. So only fail if the symbol count after decrement becomes 0 and that symbol is required. So check: if symbol in required_symbols and symbol_counts[symbol] - 1 == 0: then removal invalid. So we do that check.\n   - If valid, remove: removed_ids.add(node['id']); removal_order.append(node['id']); symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1.\n10. After loop, retained_node_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed_ids]. Sort retained_node_ids (ascending order).\n11. Return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained_node_ids}.\n\nNote: The removal_order is the order we removed (which matches processing order). That satisfies \"actual removal order\".\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we don't mutate input.\n\nTest on provided cases? But we can't run; just implement.\n\nNow produce the final JSON with key \"implementation\" and the Python source as a JSON string. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2201,"completion_tokens":2520,"total_tokens":4721,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2176},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1961},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2176,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":25},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}